<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Macedonian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">171–<a href="/years/168-bce">168 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Macedonian War was fought from 171 to 168 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a>. After early setbacks, the Roman consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a> crushed the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> at Pydna in June 168 BCE, and Perseus was taken to Rome. The Antigonid kingdom was abolished and divided into four republics under Roman supervision.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war.jpg" alt="Third Macedonian War" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-François Pierre Peyron · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois-Pierre_Peyron_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> died in 179 BCE and was succeeded by his son Perseus, who was hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Seeking allies, Perseus married Laodice, daughter of the Seleucid king Seleucus IV, and arranged for his daughter to marry Prusias II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, an enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, who was Rome's ally.</p><p>The Thracian king Abrupolis, a Roman ally, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> as far as Amphipolis and overran the gold mines of Mount Pangaeus, but Perseus drove him out of his own lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> objected to the expulsion of its ally. In 172 BCE a Roman commission demanded concessions that would have ended Macedonian independence; Perseus refused, and Rome declared war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war was fought mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. For 171 BCE the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius Licinius Crassus</a> received the command, with two legions of 6,000 men each, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a> and Thessaly sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> were divided, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> was brought into the Roman alliance. A Roman fleet besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliartus">Haliartus</a>, whose 2,500 defenders in the citadel were sold as slaves when it fell, and the town was razed. After an inconclusive battle at Callinicus, several years of campaigning followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> describes Roman commanders plundering Boeotian cities, and records that Perseus surprised the fleet at Oreum on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, capturing five warships and twenty grain transports.</p><p>In 169 BCE the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-marcius-philippus">Quintus Marcius Philippus</a> advanced towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> but fell back when supplies ran short, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> notes allegations that he feared to risk battle. Perseus lost the Illyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a> as an ally through his reluctance to pay the promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, and in 168 BCE the praetor Lucius Anicius defeated Gentius, who surrendered and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The consul Lucius Aemilius Paulus prepared his campaign carefully and met Perseus on the River Elpeus. At Pydna the fighting began by accident or design, accounts differ, over a loose mule or a horse sent across a stream. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> won decisively.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Perseus fled with his cavalry and retinue, reached Amphipolis three days after the battle and sent ambassadors to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Aemilius sent troops against towns that had revolted or killed Roman soldiers, among them Agassae and Aeginium. Epirus, which had gone over to Perseus, was pacified by Anicius, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> was split into three cantons, with cities that had deserted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a> exempted from tribute.</p><p>The victory ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonid-dynasty">Antigonid dynasty</a> and effectively ended the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, although formal annexation came some years later. The kingdom was divided into four client republics, called merides, with capitals at Amphipolis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, Pella and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a>, joined in a federal League of the Macedonians. Roman prestige and authority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> greatly increased.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Macedonian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Third Macedonian War</b> the pro-Roman politician Callicrates denounced his rivals, and about 1,000 leading Achaeans were deported to Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Third Macedonian War</b> broke out in 171 BCE; after early successes Perseus was decisively beaten at Pydna in June 168 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Third Macedonian War</b> (171–168 BCE) Rhodian envoys tried to mediate between Rome and Perseus of Macedon, which the Senate took as disloyalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Third Macedonian War</b> the Molossians sided with Perseus while other Epirotes supported Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Third Macedonian War</b> Rome separated Leucas from Acarnania in 167 BCE, and it came under Roman control.</blockquote><a href="/state/leucas">Leucas</a></li></ul></section>
